        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 822,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 691,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the Bessel function of the first kind of order 1, J1, to every\nelement of a tensor.\n\nIt gives the size of the bright rings around a point of light seen\nthrough a round lens (the Airy disk), and in FM synthesis J1 of the\nmodulation index is how loud the first pair of sidebands is.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nJ1 starts at 0, rises to its peak of about 0.582 at x = 1.84, then\noscillates with slowly shrinking swings, a quarter-cycle behind J0. Its\nzeros are 0, 3.832, 7.016, 10.17 ... It is antisymmetric: J1(-x) = -J1(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.bessel_j1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 20 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is J1(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 20,\ndrawn from -0.4 (bottom) to 0.7 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.bessel_j0 is order 0 (carrier where this is the first\nsideband). t.special.i1 is the modified version, which grows instead of\noscillating.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 675,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the Bessel function of the first kind of order 1, J1, to every\nelement of a tensor.\n\nIt gives the size of the bright rings around a point of light seen\nthrough a round lens (the Airy disk), and in FM synthesis J1 of the\nmodulation index is how loud the first pair of sidebands is.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nJ1 starts at 0, rises to its peak of about 0.582 at x = 1.84, then\noscillates with slowly shrinking swings, a quarter-cycle behind J0. Its\nzeros are 0, 3.832, 7.016, 10.17 ... It is antisymmetric: J1(-x) = -J1(x).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.bessel_j1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it\ntriggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is\nthe same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device\nas the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200\nevenly spaced values from its start to its stop (0 to 20 here), and the\nupper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is J1(x) of each\nof those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from 0 to 20,\ndrawn from -0.4 (bottom) to 0.7 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace\nto look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.bessel_j0 is order 0 (carrier where this is the first\nsideband). t.special.i1 is the modified version, which grows instead of\noscillating."
        },
